  "title": "A Day in My Life: Swahili Time",
  "category": "time",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "level": 3,
  "order": 303,
  "theme": "routine",
  "culturalNote": "Swahili time starts at sunrise, not midnight: 7 a.m. is \"saa moja asubuhi\" (the first hour of the morning), and 6 a.m. is \"saa kumi na mbili\" (the twelfth hour). The trick is to add or subtract six. Kenyans also talk about \"Kenyan time\" — a meeting at \"saa tatu\" may start closer to ten. Days of the week are counted from Saturday: Jumamosi (Sat), Jumapili (Sun), Jumatatu (Mon), Jumanne (Tue), Jumatano (Wed), Alhamisi (Thu), Ijumaa (Fri).",
  "startingPoint": "Tea break at the office. Your colleague Achieng wants to know how you manage to be first in every morning.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "time-swahili",
    "present-na",
    "question-words"
